<p>In the summer of 1863 a lame dervish arrived at the gates of Samarkand. He carried a crutch under his left arm, wore the patched cloak of a wandering holy man, and gave his name as Reshit Efendi. The ruler of the city did not believe him. He kept the dervish standing in an audience for a full half hour, watching him, waiting for the accent to slip.</p>

<p>The accent did not slip. The man left the audience carrying gifts.</p>

<p>He was not a dervish and his name was not Reshit. He was Ármin Vámbéry, a Hungarian who had been born in poverty, left school at twelve, and taught himself Turkish by drawing the letters in sand with a stick. He had spent two years walking through Central Asia in disguise, and he had not written a single note on the way, because a scrap of paper in a European hand would have ended him.</p>

<p>He is the clearest case in this archive of a specific and unfashionable idea: that a language is not learned by studying it but by needing it, and that the memory can be trained until it does the work a notebook would otherwise do.</p>

<h2>Szentgyörgy, 1832</h2>

<p>He was born on 19 March 1832 in Szentgyörgy, in Pozsony County of the Kingdom of Hungary - now Svätý Jur in Slovakia - into a poor Jewish family. His original name was Hermann Wamberger. His father died while he was an infant, and his mother remarried and moved the family to Dunaszerdahely, now Dunajská Streda.</p>

<p>The household economy, as he describes it in his own autobiography, was leeches. His stepfather hawked them; bleeding was still fashionable in Hungary, and the family's fortunes rose and fell with the demand for a medical practice that was already dying. In lean weeks they ate black bread, potatoes, beans, peas and lentils, with meat in small portions on Saturdays and feast days. Coffee and milk were luxuries.</p>

<p>He also had a congenital lameness, which he says began to trouble him at three and which required a crutch under his left arm until his mother declared him grown. Later, walking across Persia and Turkestan, he would regard the childhood he describes - constant hunger, scanty clothing, no familiarity with comfort - as training. It had hardened a body that needed hardening.</p>

